theory.intro:
A **unit circle** is a circle centred at the origin $O$ with radius $1$. The point $P$ at angle $\theta$, measured anticlockwise from the positive $x$-axis, has coordinates $(\cos\theta,\sin\theta)$. For the illustrated first-quadrant acute angle, the radius is the hypotenuse, so sine and cosine are just the corresponding coordinates. These coordinate definitions extend sine and cosine to all angles: ordinary side lengths cannot equal negative coordinates in other quadrants, and the construction gives no nondegenerate right triangle at boundary angles.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\usetikzlibrary{angles, quotes, calc}
\coordinate (O) at (0,0);
\coordinate (X) at (1.2,0);
\coordinate (P) at (55:2.4);
\coordinate (F) at ({2.4*cos(55)},0);
\draw[{Stealth}-{Stealth}] (-3,0)--(3,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[{Stealth}-{Stealth}] (0,-3)--(0,3) node[above] {$y$};
\draw (O) circle (2.4);
\draw[thick] (O)--(P) node[midway, above left] {$1$};
\draw[red, thick] (F)--(P) node[midway, right] {$y$};
\draw[blue, thick] (O)--(F) node[midway, below] {$x$};
\fill (P) circle (1.6pt);
\node[above right] at (P) {$P(x,y)$};
\node[below left] at (O) {$O$};
\node[below] at (2.4,0) {$1$};
\node[below] at (-2.4,0) {$-1$};
\node[left] at (0,2.4) {$1$};
\node[left] at (0,-2.4) {$-1$};
\pic[draw, "$\theta$", angle radius=0.75cm, angle eccentricity=1.5] {angle=X--O--P};
\coordinate (ra) at ($(F)!0.28cm!(O)$);
\coordinate (rb) at ($(F)!0.28cm!(P)$);
\draw (ra) -- ($(ra)+(rb)-(F)$) -- (rb);
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

